-- Lesson 1: Why BODMAS/PEMDAS?
'{"hook":"Is the answer to 2 + 3 × 4 equal to 20 or 14? Without an agreed order of operations, two people could get different answers to the same problem — and bridges could collapse.","inventor":{"name":"Mathematical Convention","birth_year":null,"death_year":null,"nationality":"International","bio":"The order of operations was not invented by one person. It evolved gradually from the 1500s to 1900s as mathematical notation became standardized. The need arose because algebraic expressions became ambiguous without agreed conventions."},"year_invented":1600,"story_paragraphs":["Imagine two engineers calculating the load on a bridge beam. One computes 2 + 3 × 4 = 20 (left to right), the other gets 14 (multiplication first). They can''t both be right — and the wrong answer could be catastrophic.","As algebra grew more complex in the 1500s-1600s, mathematicians realized they needed universal rules. Multiplication and division were given priority over addition and subtraction because they are ''stronger'' operations — repeated addition.","The specific acronyms came later. BODMAS (Brackets, Orders, Division, Multiplication, Addition, Subtraction) is used in UK, Australia, and India. PEMDAS (Parentheses, Exponents, Multiplication, Division, Addition, Subtraction) is used in the US. They describe the same rules.","Today, every calculator, computer, and spreadsheet follows these rules. Programming languages, scientific formulas, and financial calculations all depend on everyone agreeing on the same order. It''s one of mathematics'' most important social contracts."],"key_contributors":[{"name":"Niccolò Tartaglia","contribution":"Italian mathematician (1500s) whose algebraic notation helped establish operation precedence"},{"name":"Leonhard Euler","contribution":"Standardized much of modern mathematical notation in the 1700s"}],"real_world_applications":[{"name":"Calculators","icon":"🔢","description":"Every calculator follows order of operations — try 2 + 3 × 4 on yours"},{"name":"Spreadsheets","icon":"📊","description":"Excel and Google Sheets use order of operations in every formula"},{"name":"Programming","icon":"💻","description":"Every programming language follows operator precedence rules based on BODMAS"},{"name":"Engineering","icon":"🏗️","description":"Structural calculations MUST be unambiguous — wrong order = wrong result = danger"}],"image":{"url":null,"alt":"Mathematical expression showing order of operations","attribution":"Wikimedia Commons, public domain","source_url":null}}'::jsonb)
